{"path":"s11","heading":"Appointment of arbitrators.","in_1996":true,"nodes_1996":[{"path":"s11","node_type":"section","number":"11","heading":"Appointment of arbitrators.","text":""},{"path":"s11.ss1","node_type":"subsection","number":"1","heading":null,"text":"A person of any nationality may be an arbitrator, unless otherwise agreed by the parties."},{"path":"s11.ss2","node_type":"subsection","number":"2","heading":null,"text":"Subject to sub-section (6), the parties are free to agree on a procedure for appointing the arbitrator or arbitrators."},{"path":"s11.ss3","node_type":"subsection","number":"3","heading":null,"text":"Failing any agreement referred to in sub-section (2), in an arbitration with three arbitrators, each party shall appoint one arbitrator, and the two appointed arbitrators shall appoint the third arbitrator who shall act as the presiding arbitrator."},{"path":"s11.ss4","node_type":"subsection","number":"4","heading":null,"text":"If the appointment procedure in sub-section (3) applies and—\nthe appointment shall be made, upon request of a party, by the Chief Justice or any person or institution designated by him."},{"path":"s11.ss4.cl(a)","node_type":"clause","number":"a","heading":null,"text":"a party fails to appoint an arbitrator within thirty days from the receipt of a request to do so from the other party; or"},{"path":"s11.ss4.cl(b)","node_type":"clause","number":"b","heading":null,"text":"the two appointed arbitrators fail to agree on the third arbitrator within thirty days from the date of their appointment,"},{"path":"s11.ss5","node_type":"subsection","number":"5","heading":null,"text":"Failing any agreement referred to in sub-section (2), in an arbitration with a sole arbitrator, if the parties fail to agree on the arbitrator within thirty days from receipt of a request by one party from the other party to so agree the appointment shall be made, upon request of a party, by the Chief Justice or any person or institution designated by him."},{"path":"s11.ss6","node_type":"subsection","number":"6","heading":null,"text":"Where, under an appointment procedure agreed upon by the parties,—\na party may request the Chief Justice or any person or institution designated by him to take the necessary measure, unless the agreement on the appointment procedure provides other means for securing the appointment."},{"path":"s11.ss6.cl(a)","node_type":"clause","number":"a","heading":null,"text":"a party fails to act as required under that procedure; or"},{"path":"s11.ss6.cl(b)","node_type":"clause","number":"b","heading":null,"text":"the parties, or the two appointed arbitrators, fail to reach an agreement expected of them under that procedure; or"},{"path":"s11.ss6.cl(c)","node_type":"clause","number":"c","heading":null,"text":"a person, including an institution, fails to perform any function entrusted to him or it under that procedure,"},{"path":"s11.ss7","node_type":"subsection","number":"7","heading":null,"text":"A decision on a matter entrusted by sub-section (4) or sub-section (5) or sub-section (6) to the Chief Justice or the person or institution designated by him is final."},{"path":"s11.ss8","node_type":"subsection","number":"8","heading":null,"text":"The Chief Justice or the person or institution designated by him, in appointing an arbitrator, shall have due regard to—"},{"path":"s11.ss8.cl(a)","node_type":"clause","number":"a","heading":null,"text":"any qualifications required of the arbitrator by the agreement of the parties; and"},{"path":"s11.ss8.cl(b)","node_type":"clause","number":"b","heading":null,"text":"other considerations as are likely to secure the appointment of an independent and impartial arbitrator."},{"path":"s11.ss9","node_type":"subsection","number":"9","heading":null,"text":"In the case of appointment of sole or third arbitrator in an international commercial arbitration, the Chief Justice of India or the person or institution designated by him may appoint an arbitrator of a nationality other than the nationalities of the parties where the parties belong to different nationalities."},{"path":"s11.ss10","node_type":"subsection","number":"10","heading":null,"text":"The Chief Justice may make such scheme as he may deem appropriate for dealing with matters entrusted by sub-section (4) or sub-section (5) or sub-section (6) to him."},{"path":"s11.ss11","node_type":"subsection","number":"11","heading":null,"text":"Where more than one request has been made under sub-section (4) or sub-section (5) or sub-section (6) to the Chief Justices of different High Courts or their designates, the Chief Justice or his designate to whom the request has been first made under the relevant sub-section shall alone be competent to decide on the request."},{"path":"s11.ss12","node_type":"subsection","number":"12","heading":null,"text":""},{"path":"s11.ss12.cl(a)","node_type":"clause","number":"a","heading":null,"text":"Where the matters referred to in sub-sections (4), (5), (6), (7), (8) and (10) arise in an international commercial arbitration the reference to “Chief Justice” in those sub-section shall be construed as a reference to the “Chief Justice of India.”"},{"path":"s11.ss12.cl(b)","node_type":"clause","number":"b","heading":null,"text":"Where the matter referred to in sub-sections (4), (5) (6), (7), (8), and (10) arise in any other arbitration, the reference to “Chief Justice” in those sub-sections shall be construed as a reference to, the Chief Justice of the High Court within whose local limits the principal Civil Court referred to in clause (e) of sub-section (1) of section 2 is situate and, where the High Court itself is the Court referred to in that clause, to the Chief justice of that High Court."}],"events":[{"instrument":"3/2016","title":"Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) Act, 2015 (No. 3 of 2016)","effective":"from 23 Oct 2015 (Ordinance 9 of 2015)","edges":[{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss4","new":"s11.ss4","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(i)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss5","new":"s11.ss5","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(i)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss6","new":"s11.ss6","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(i)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":null,"new":"s11.ss6A","op_type":"insert","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(ii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":null,"new":"s11.ss6B","op_type":"insert","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(ii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss7","new":"s11.ss7","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(iii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss8","new":"s11.ss8","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(iv)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":null,"new":"s11.ss13","op_type":"insert","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(ix)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":null,"new":"s11.ss14","op_type":"insert","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(ix)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss9","new":"s11.ss9","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(v)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss10","new":"s11.ss10","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(vi)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss11","new":"s11.ss11","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(vii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"3/2016","old":"s11.ss12","new":"s11.ss12","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"3/2016|s6.cl(viii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null}],"instructions":{"3/2016|s6.cl(i)":"in sub-sections (4), (5) and (6), for the words “the Chief Justice or any person or institution designated by him” wherever they occur, the words “the Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court or any person or institution designated by such Court” shall be substituted;","3/2016|s6.cl(ii)":"after sub-section (6), the following sub-sections shall be inserted, namely:— “(6A) The Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court, while considering any application under sub-section (4) or sub-section (5) or sub-section (6), shall, notwithstanding any judgment, decree or order of any Court, confine to the examination of the existence of an arbitration agreement. (6B) The designation of any person or institution by the Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court, for the purposes of this section shall not be regarded as a delegation of judicial power by the Supreme Court or the High Court.”;","3/2016|s6.cl(iii)":"in sub-section (7), for the words “the Chief Justice or the person or institution designated by him is final”, the words “the Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court or the person or institution designated by such Court is final and no appeal including Letters Patent Appeal shall lie against such decision” shall be substituted;","3/2016|s6.cl(iv)":"for sub-section (8), the following sub-section shall be substituted, namely:— “(8) The Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court or the person or institution designated by such Court, before appointing an arbitrator, shall seek a disclosure in writing from the prospective arbitrator in terms of sub-section (1) of section 12, and have due regard to— (a) any qualifications required for the arbitrator by the agreement of the parties; and (b) the contents of the disclosure and other considerations as are likely to secure the appointment of an independent and impartial arbitrator.”;","3/2016|s6.cl(ix)":"after sub-section (12), the following sub-sections shall be inserted, namely:— “(13) An application made under this section for appointment of an arbitrator or arbitrators shall be disposed of by the Supreme Court or the High Court or the person or institution designated by such Court, as the case may be, as expeditiously as possible and an endeavour shall be made to dispose of the matter within a period of sixty days from the date of service of notice on the opposite party. (14) For the purpose of determination of the fees of the arbitral tribunal and the manner of its payment to the arbitral tribunal, the High Court may frame such rules as may be necessary, after taking into consideration the rates specified in the Fourth Schedule. Explanation.—For the removal of doubts, it is hereby clarified that this sub-section shall not apply to international commercial arbitration and in arbitrations (other than international commercial arbitration) in case where parties have agreed for determination of fees as per the rules of an arbitral institution.’’.","3/2016|s6.cl(v)":"in sub-section (9), for the words “the Chief Justice of India or the person or institution designated by him”, the words “the Supreme Court or the person or institution designated by that Court” shall be substituted;","3/2016|s6.cl(vi)":"for sub-section (10), the following sub-section shall be substituted, namely:— “(10) The Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court, may make such scheme as the said Court may deem appropriate for dealing with matters entrusted by sub-section (4) or sub-section (5) or sub-section (6), to it.”;","3/2016|s6.cl(vii)":"in sub-section (11), for the words “the Chief Justices of different High Courts or their designates, the Chief Justice or his designate to whom the request has been first made”, the words “different High Courts or their designates, the High Court or its designate to whom the request has been first made” shall be substituted;","3/2016|s6.cl(viii)":"for sub-section (12), the following sub-section shall be substituted, namely:— ‘(12) (a) Where the matters referred to in sub-sections (4), (5), (6), (7), (8) and sub-section (10) arise in an international commercial arbitration, the reference to the “Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court” in those sub-sections shall be construed as a reference to the “Supreme Court”; and (b) where the matters referred to in sub-sections (4), (5), (6), (7), (8) and sub-section (10) arise in any other arbitration, the reference to “the Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court” in those sub-sections shall be construed as a reference to the “High Court” within whose local limits the principal Civil Court referred to in clause (e) of sub-section (1) of section 2 is situate, and where the High Court itself is the Court referred to in that clause, to that High Court.’;"},"intent":[{"unit":"3/2016|s6","status":"sourced","claim":"The amendment rationalizes the law and provides greater incentive for the High Court and/or Supreme Court to delegate the power of appointment to specialized, external persons or institutions.","tier":"report","quote":"This would rationalize the law and provide greater incentive for the\nHigh Court and/or Supreme Court to delegate the power of appointment\n(being a non-judicial act) to specialized, external persons or institutions.","document_id":59,"span":[42392,42606],"attribution":{"by":"Law Commission of India, Report No. 246","role":"committee","where":"Law Commission of India, Report No. 246: Amendments to the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 (August 2014), part 1"},"tiers_searched":["sor","debate","report"]}]},{"instrument":"33/2019","title":"Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) Act, 2019 (No. 33 of 2019)","effective":"in part: ss. 1, 4–9, 11–13, 15 from 30 Aug 2019 (S.O. 3154(E)); other sections by later notification or not commenced","edges":[{"instrument":"33/2019","old":null,"new":"s11.ss3A","op_type":"insert","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(i)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss4","new":"s11.ss4","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(ii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss5","new":"s11.ss5","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(iii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss6","new":"s11.ss6","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(iv)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss11","new":"s11.ss11","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(ix)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss12","new":"s11.ss12","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(ix)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss13","new":"s11.ss13","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(ix)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss14","new":"s11.ss14","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(ix)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss6A","new":null,"op_type":"omit","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(v)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss7","new":null,"op_type":"omit","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(v)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss8","new":"s11.ss8","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(vi)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss9","new":"s11.ss9","op_type":"substitute","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(vii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null},{"instrument":"33/2019","old":"s11.ss10","new":null,"op_type":"omit","source":"rule","group":"33/2019|s3.cl(viii)","confidence":1.0,"rationale":null}],"instructions":{"33/2019|s3.cl(i)":"after sub-section (3), the following sub-section shall be inserted, namely:— “(3A) The Supreme Court and the High Court shall have the power to designate, arbitral institutions, from time to time, which have been graded by the Council under section 43-I, for the purposes of this Act: Provided that in respect of those High Court jurisdictions, where no graded arbitral institution are available, then, the Chief Justice of the concerned High Court may maintain a panel of arbitrators for discharging the functions and duties of arbitral institution and any reference to the arbitrator shall be deemed to be an arbitral institution for the purposes of this section and the arbitrator appointed by a party shall be entitled to such fee at the rate as specified in the Fourth Schedule: Provided further that the Chief Justice of the concerned High Court may, from time to time, review the panel of arbitrators.”;","33/2019|s3.cl(ii)":"in sub-section (4), in the long line, for the portion beginning with “the appointment shall be made” and ending with ‘‘designated by such Court”, the following shall be substituted, namely:— “the appointment shall be made, on an application of the party, by the arbitral institution designated by the Supreme Court, in case of international commercial arbitration, or by the High Court, in case of arbitrations other than international commercial arbitration, as the case may be”;","33/2019|s3.cl(iii)":"in sub-section (5), for the portion beginning with ‘‘the appointment shall be made” and ending with ‘‘designated by such Court”, the following shall be substituted, namely:— “the appointment shall be made on an application of the party in accordance with the provisions contained in sub-section (4)”;","33/2019|s3.cl(iv)":"in sub-section (6), in the long line, for the portion beginning with ‘‘party may request” and ending with ‘‘designated by such Court”, the following shall be substituted, namely:— “the appointment shall be made, on an application of the party, by the arbitral institution designated by the Supreme Court, in case of international commercial arbitration, or by the High Court, in case of arbitrations other than international commercial arbitration, as the case may be”;","33/2019|s3.cl(ix)":"for sub-sections (11) to (14), the following sub-sections shall be substituted, namely:— “(11) Where more than one request has been made under sub-section (4) or sub-section (5) or sub-section (6) to different arbitral institutions, the arbitral institution to which the request has been first made under the relevant sub-section shall be competent to appoint. (12) Where the matter referred to in sub-sections (4), (5), (6) and (8) arise in an international commercial arbitration or any other arbitration, the reference to the arbitral institution in those sub-sections shall be construed as a reference to the arbitral institution designated under sub-section (3A). (13) An application made under this section for appointment of an arbitrator or arbitrators shall be disposed of by the arbitral institution within a period of thirty days from the date of service of notice on the opposite party. (14) The arbitral institution shall determine the fees of the arbitral tribunal and the manner of its payment to the arbitral tribunal subject to the rates specified in the Fourth Schedule. Explanation.—For the removal of doubts, it is hereby clarified that this sub-section shall not apply to international commercial arbitration and in arbitrations (other than international commercial arbitration) where parties have agreed for determination of fees as per the rules of an arbitral institution.”.","33/2019|s3.cl(v)":"sub-sections (6A) and (7) shall be omitted;","33/2019|s3.cl(vi)":"in sub-section (8), for the words “The Supreme Court or, as the case may be, the High Court or the person or institution designated by such Court”, the words, brackets and figures “The arbitral institution referred to in sub-sections (4), (5) and (6)” shall be substituted;","33/2019|s3.cl(vii)":"in sub-section (9), for the words “the Supreme Court or the person or institution designated by that Court”, the words “the arbitral institution designated by the Supreme Court” shall be substituted;","33/2019|s3.cl(viii)":"sub-section (10) shall be omitted;"},"intent":[{"unit":"33/2019|s3","status":"sourced","claim":"The amendment was made to reduce reliance on courts for appointing arbitrators and to promote institutional arbitration, aiming to make India a hub for such arbitration.","tier":"sor","quote":"The Committee has also recommended certain amendments\nto the said Act to minimise the need to approach the Courts for appointment of arbitrators.\nAfter examination of the said recommendations with a view to make India a hub of institutional\narbitration for both domestic and international arbitration, it was decided to amend the\nArbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.","document_id":28,"span":[26300,26669],"attribution":{"by":"Government","role":"government","where":"Statement of Objects and Reasons, The Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) Bill, 2019 (as introduced in Rajya Sabha)","signed_by":"RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD"},"tiers_searched":["sor"]}]}]}